<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean de Brébeuf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1593">1593</a>–<a href="/years/1649">1649</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean de Brébeuf was a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary who worked among the Huron (Wendat) from 1626, learning their language and writing a dictionary and catechism. He also visited the Neutral confederacy in 1640–1641. In 1649, when the Iroquois destroyed the Huron towns, he was captured at Saint-Louis and tortured to death. He is venerated as one of the Canadian Martyrs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-de-brebeuf.jpg" alt="Jean de Brébeuf" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thwaites, Reuben Gold, 1853-1913 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Br%C3%A9buef-jesuits04jesuuoft.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jean de Brébeuf was born on 25 March 1593 at Condé-sur-Vire in Normandy and joined the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in 1617, at the age of 24. From 1619 to 1621 he taught at the college of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>. In 1620 he contracted tuberculosis and was nearly expelled from the Society, since the illness prevented him from studying and teaching for the usual periods. His record as a student was not particularly distinguished, but he already showed an aptitude for languages. He was ordained a priest at Pontoise Cathedral in February 1622 and then spent three years as steward of the college at Rouen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Brébeuf arrived in Québec in June 1625. He lived for about five months with a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innu">Innu</a> and in 1626 was assigned to the Wendat, who spoke an Iroquoian language. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> surrendered to the English on 19 July 1629, the missionaries returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Brébeuf returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> in 1633. With Antoine Daniel and Ambroise Davost he chose Ihonatiria as the centre of the mission. The Wendat, who called him Échon, were suffering epidemics of Eurasian diseases, and nearly half of them died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> by 1640. Many blamed the Europeans, and many believed Brébeuf was a sorcerer.</p><p>He considered Wendat beliefs undeveloped, yet he compiled a detailed ethnographic record and in 1636 wrote an account of the Feast of the Dead. He discovered the compound words of Wendat, translated Ledesma's catechism, the first printed text in the language, and compiled a dictionary. He is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huron-carol">Huron Carol</a>, written about 1642.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 16 March 1649 the Haudenosaunee destroyed the Wendat mission village of Saint-Louis and took Brébeuf and Gabriel Lalemant to Taenhatenteron, also called St. Ignace. There, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> accounts drawn from Wendat refugees who had escaped, they were ritually tortured and killed, and the Haudenosaunee ate his heart. His remains were recovered and sent to Québec, where his skull was kept in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> reliquary. He was beatified in 1925 and canonised in 1930 with eight other Jesuit missionaries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean de Brébeuf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Carols grew in popularity after the Reformation, when reformers such as Martin Luther promoted congregational singing, and they travelled with missions: the Huron Carol, counted as Canada's oldest Christmas song, was written, probably in 1642, by the Jesuit <b>Jean de Brébeuf</b> in the language of the Huron-Wendat people.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Huron Carol was written, probably in 1642, by the Jesuit <b>Jean de Brébeuf</b> at Sainte-Marie among the Hurons, in the language of the Huron-Wendat people and to the tune of a French folk song.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>It was written, probably in 1642, by <b>Jean de Brébeuf</b>, a Jesuit missionary at Sainte-Marie among the Hurons, in the language of the Huron-Wendat people.</blockquote><a href="/songs/huron-carol">Huron Carol</a></li><li><blockquote>Epidemics in the 1630s halved the population, and in 1648 to 1649 the Iroquois Confederacy destroyed the main towns and killed missionaries such as <b>Jean de Brébeuf</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/huron-confederacy">Huron Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Recollet and Jesuit missionaries, among them <b>Jean de Brébeuf</b> in 1640 to 1641, made few converts.</blockquote><a href="/state/neutral-confederacy">Neutral Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Condé-sur-Vire, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Georgian Bay, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.05,-1.04&amp;year=1629" aria-label="Condé-sur-Vire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jean de Brébeuf"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4942 5930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4942 5930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.05,-1.04&amp;year=1629">What was here in 1629? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
